Stage 2 confirms byte-for-byte translation fidelity. Across 318 positive inputs, the candidate matches an independent exact-integer contract oracle with zero mismatches, including loop-exit, odd/even branch boundaries, the documented example, random values, and large integers. The trusted canonical diverges on three values above the float-exactness boundary because it uses `/`; I’ll record that as a canonical implementation discrepancy, not as evidence that the candidate violates the natural-language integer contract. Next I’m rebuilding both K definitions from only source and the trusted semantics.
The trusted translator regenerated solution.mpy byte-identically. Independent differential testing found zero candidate-versus-contract mismatches; the trusted canonical has three float-rounding divergences on very large positive integers.
  ✓ Stage 1: verify launcher provenance, record layout, trusted mounts, hashes, and supplied-semantics integrity
  ✓ Stage 2: check source/translation fidelity and run independent differential tests
  → Stage 3: reconstruct semantics and proofs from source in clean scratch and run all positive claims
  • Stage 4: audit claim adequacy, real-program pinning, and concrete satisfying witnesses
  • Stage 5: inventory and review every syntax item, function, declaration, rule, and simplification
  • Stage 6: create and run a fresh meaningful false-result mutation
  • Stage 7: account for proven versus assumed facts and write the evidence-backed verdict
