Adequacy is the material failure: the embedded module and closure mechanically parse to the exact submitted `solution.mpy`, and a body mutation makes `case-5` fail with the expected wrong heap, so the fixed examples genuinely execute the candidate. But `spec.k` has no symbolic end-to-end entry claim: its only entry arguments are `1, 5, 6, 7`; `collatzResult` is never referenced by any claim; and the three universal claims establish one odd step, one even step, and the exit tail separately. Their composition into an unrestricted theorem exists only in prose, not as a K reachability proof. I’m continuing with the required exhaustive rule audit and mutation gate before assigning the final benchmark verdict.
Mechanical KAST comparison pins the fixed entry claims to the exact submitted module/closure, and a real-body mutation was rejected. However, no claim executes #getOddCollatz on symbolic N; only inputs 1, 5, 6, and 7 have result-constraining end-to-end claims, while collatzResult is unused by spec.k.
